      "question": {
        ":question/id": "a3ba0aff-c78a-5660-8c3a-3468d7d8534d",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/problem": "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}{2 x}$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "In this case, the expression for $y$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $v(x)=2 x.$\n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}(x)&=\\frac{u^{\\prime}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot v^{\\prime}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{(2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})^{\\prime}\\cdot (2 x)-(2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (2 x)^{\\prime}}{(2 x)^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{(2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (2 x)-(2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (2)}{(2 x)^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{4(x\\cos x-\\sin x)}{4x^2}\\\\ &=\\frac{2 x \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}{2x^2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\pi$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}(\\pi)&=\\frac{2 (\\pi) \\cos{\\left((\\pi) \\right)} - 2 \\sin{\\left((\\pi) \\right)}}{2(\\pi)^2}\\\\ &=\\frac{- 2 \\pi}{2 \\pi^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{- 2 \\pi}{2 \\pi^{2}}\\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{\\pi}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $- \\frac{1}{\\pi}.$",
